[[:db/retract 17592186134132 :question/answer-fields 17592186130948] {:db/id 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7" :question/id #uuid "598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162231" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ff271c5-90b5-5fb1-aa9a-8de6b8aed244"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ff271c5-90b5-5fb1-aa9a-8de6b8aed244" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff271c5-90b5-5fb1-aa9a-8de6b8aed244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81f383ab-ca0f-54cf-8cd9-153255df4842" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4543906-53e0-543b-8b49-5cef8732311e" "answer-82168b66-7a44-5eb9-8fa3-a04f1522bfc1" "answer-77c7dfa0-202b-5af6-9228-dc6355dcb12c" "answer-81f383ab-ca0f-54cf-8cd9-153255df4842" "answer-ac321221-fe9b-5704-a522-fd0ed1517250"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4543906-53e0-543b-8b49-5cef8732311e" :answer/id #uuid "c4543906-53e0-543b-8b49-5cef8732311e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82168b66-7a44-5eb9-8fa3-a04f1522bfc1" :answer/id #uuid "82168b66-7a44-5eb9-8fa3-a04f1522bfc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77c7dfa0-202b-5af6-9228-dc6355dcb12c" :answer/id #uuid "77c7dfa0-202b-5af6-9228-dc6355dcb12c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81f383ab-ca0f-54cf-8cd9-153255df4842" :answer/id #uuid "81f383ab-ca0f-54cf-8cd9-153255df4842" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac321221-fe9b-5704-a522-fd0ed1517250" :answer/id #uuid "ac321221-fe9b-5704-a522-fd0ed1517250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134133 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(y-x)\\,i$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{3}-t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t-{t}^{2}+t)\\,i \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,i\\,)\\cdot (\\,(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k\\,) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,2{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{3})-(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130954 :answer-field/correct 17592186130955 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" "question-580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105580 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4) .$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4,$ so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(8) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13360da3-e828-58d3-a8ca-a868355e39a5" "question-c386627f-74f0-5d5a-9a11-2fd0a0613fa9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105581 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y =  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right).$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105726 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20f5e3df-f068-575a-bd88-8cc274ff8c19" "question-94ed6f2f-e6d7-5eca-a61d-41902b4a3cb4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105583 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105725 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29481c76-b7fa-575a-9ace-40d060b32844" "question-1d09c144-84e6-5d74-866a-0f1eb2fa303b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105582 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" :question/id #uuid "d5bc96f7-1e4b-5204-9506-eaed8cba8e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-339829" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "52a6db62-005f-550b-8c07-96f59d9e4b2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/id #uuid "e60bc47c-39ab-535f-af0a-0114fb9b73a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20" :question/id #uuid "580b167f-860a-55e7-8509-7c52af963b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-54470" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5806411-08d6-5ddd-b363-c73129ba0cfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c90079e-1664-5315-a4e8-b23619bb6336" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" "answer-2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" "answer-8c90079e-1664-5315-a4e8-b23619bb6336" "answer-cf438cbb-e1b4-525e-bb7c-32d71029f2b6" "answer-36fddc55-77af-52ed-88ba-e95230bef581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" :answer/id #uuid "a1c2ea8b-9131-5dd2-9555-9cdde1b9d662" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" :answer/id #uuid "2446d45d-0402-58a6-91cf-846d84f2cdb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c90079e-1664-53