\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55bae5ab-9b0c-588d-9a47-033bbbf8ef6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55bae5ab-9b0c-588d-9a47-033bbbf8ef6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "55bae5ab-9b0c-588d-9a47-033bbbf8ef6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48108c9a-7393-5a6d-a4b9-1ee7f3a4618c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-562d9cc7-78ed-57a2-b9df-268efd4fd357" "answer-3235f6d8-fc17-53d1-96a2-97ded9b47bca" "answer-1fa4fcc7-abb5-598a-86aa-535a13940109" "answer-48108c9a-7393-5a6d-a4b9-1ee7f3a4618c" "answer-d9dad321-6574-5221-b21b-bdaa701883b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-562d9cc7-78ed-57a2-b9df-268efd4fd357" :answer/id #uuid "562d9cc7-78ed-57a2-b9df-268efd4fd357" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3235f6d8-fc17-53d1-96a2-97ded9b47bca" :answer/id #uuid "3235f6d8-fc17-53d1-96a2-97ded9b47bca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy\\,\\text{d}x+2y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa4fcc7-abb5-598a-86aa-535a13940109" :answer/id #uuid "1fa4fcc7-abb5-598a-86aa-535a13940109" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48108c9a-7393-5a6d-a4b9-1ee7f3a4618c" :answer/id #uuid "48108c9a-7393-5a6d-a4b9-1ee7f3a4618c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y\\,\\text{d}x+(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9dad321-6574-5221-b21b-bdaa701883b0" :answer/id #uuid "d9dad321-6574-5221-b21b-bdaa701883b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y\\,\\text{d}x+2y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186175666 :answer-field/choices 17592186175667] [:db/retract 17592186175666 :answer-field/choices 17592186175668] [:db/retract 17592186175666 :answer-field/choices 17592186175669] [:db/retract 17592186175666 :answer-field/choices 17592186175670] [:db/retract 17592186175666 :answer-field/choices 17592186175671] {:db/id 17592186176088 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176588 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,2),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2-0}{2-0}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175666 :answer-field/correct "answer-3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51381d98-4b2c-5d35-b328-a422e2a36439" "answer-53c0aaa8-38d2-5451-8a27-0bab44551579" "answer-175bf4de-4457-5893-9e70-48b016ba1c87" "answer-3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" "answer-9049bcb9-bcf9-552e-88ca-aa47961cecd9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51381d98-4b2c-5d35-b328-a422e2a36439" :answer/id #uuid "51381d98-4b2c-5d35-b328-a422e2a36439" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53c0aaa8-38d2-5451-8a27-0bab44551579" :answer/id #uuid "53c0aaa8-38d2-5451-8a27-0bab44551579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-175bf4de-4457-5893-9e70-48b016ba1c87" :answer/id #uuid "175bf4de-4457-5893-9e70-48b016ba1c87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" :answer/id #uuid "3cc44505-66d5-5275-ab67-b9b56ad13597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9049bcb9-bcf9-552e-88ca-aa47961cecd9" :answer/id #uuid "9049bcb9-bcf9-552e-88ca-aa47961cecd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742" :question/id #uuid "7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742" :question/math-academy-id "q-97069" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(2,-6)$ and $(-1,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-2}=-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfa28e82-e7a7-5631-a9aa-0b573881c151"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfa28e82-e7a7-5631-a9aa-0b573881c151" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa28e82-e7a7-5631-a9aa-0b573881c151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aee37cb9-5834-51b9-a3fe-6104df070ec9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-620a4056-288f-5313-ad88-b1b0f5b5687b" "answer-aee37cb9-5834-51b9-a3fe-6104df070ec9" "answer-63ce0689-59e9-51d3-8840-c15ea78dfa8f" "answer-a12b6a7f-7604-5711-86fc-b635e4b043ba" "answer-1dd0d15c-be74-50b4-a48c-b8617532b07b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-620a4056-288f-5313-ad88-b1b0f5b5687b" :answer/id #uuid "620a4056-288f-5313-ad88-b1b0f5b5687b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aee37cb9-5834-51b9-a3fe-6104df070ec9" :answer/id #uuid "aee37cb9-5834-51b9-a3fe-6104df070ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63ce0689-59e9-51d3-8840-c15ea78dfa8f" :answer/id #uuid "63ce0689-59e9-51d3-8840-c15ea78dfa8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a12b6a7f-7604-5711-86fc-b635e4b043ba" :answer/id #uuid "a12b6a7f-7604-5711-86fc-b635e4b043ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dd0d15c-be74-50b4-a48c-b8617532b07b" :answer/id #uuid "1dd0d15c-be74-50b4-a48c-b8617532b07b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176273 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=0$\n\nIII. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173896 :answer-field/correct 17592186173900 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9ea48e0e-20fc-57ae-8fc6-7c2c77548e20" :activity/id #uuid "9ea48e0e-20fc-57ae-8fc6-7c2c77548e20" :activity/title "The Image of a Linear Transformation in the Cartesian Plane" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049895 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-1855919c-4174-5759-93cf-11a0280966ca" "step-521a6f9a-99a3-57bc-b710-6c05998470da" "step-cea9ce61-7dee-5a91-9c27-e1a1933f13fb" "step-af32582e-7c99-52e2-a82b-8a159c337dcb" "step-33095b71-1333-5602-a459-f3798f52eba0"] :activity/first-step "step-1855919c-4174-5759-93cf-11a0280966ca" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1855919c-4174-5759-93cf-11a0280966ca" :step/id #uuid "1855919c-4174-5759-93cf-11a0280966ca" :step/content 17592186176588 :step/next "step-521a6f9a-99a3-57bc-b710-6c05998470da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-521a6f9a-99a3-57bc-b710-6c05998470da" :step/id #uuid "521a6f9a-99a3-57bc-b710-6c05998470da" :step/content "question-7d1c2efa-cbed-55e3-967d-daf6bf46e742" :step/next "step-cea9ce61-7dee-5a91-9c27-e1a1933f13fb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cea9ce61-7dee-5a91-9c27-e1a1933f13fb" :step/id #uuid "cea9ce61-7dee-5a91-9c27-e1a1933f13fb" :step/content 17592186382648 :step/next "step-af32582e-7c99-52e2-a82b-8a159c337dcb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-af32582e-7c99-52e2-a82b-8a159c337dcb" :step/id #uuid "af32582e-7c99-52e2-a82b-8a159c337dcb" :step/content 17592186398081 :step/next "step-33095b71-1333-5602-a459-f3798f52eba0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33095b71-1333-5602-a459-f3798f52eba0" :step/id #uuid "33095b71-1333-5602-a459-f3798f52eba0" :step/content 17592186176273 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" "question-d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" :question/id #uuid "90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37587" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1df7676f-854b-544e-891a-356915ca7afe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1df7676f-854b-544e-891a-356915ca7afe" :answer-field/id #uuid "1df7676f-854b-544e-891a-356915ca7afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3108d9ea-bf52-50bb-b021-c32e5e1cac89" "answer-26e486e8-edca-5347-8954-f90b314bfea9" "answer-f422fa0c-c176-5bb8-b7d5-4e0f06f500ca" "answer-12262de4-ff35-5b6d-902b-cc063519daf3" "answer-9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3108d9ea-bf52-50bb-b021-c32e5e1cac89" :answer/id #uuid "3108d9ea-bf52-50bb-b021-c32e5e1cac89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26e486e8-edca-5347-8954-f90b314bfea9" :answer/id #uuid "26e486e8-edca-5347-8954-f90b314bfea9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f422fa0c-c176-5bb8-b7d5-4e0f06f500ca" :answer/id #uuid "f422fa0c-c176-5bb8-b7d5-4e0f06f500ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12262de4-ff35-5b6d-902b-cc063519daf3" :answer/id #uuid "12262de4-ff35-5b6d-902b-cc063519daf3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d" :answer/id #uuid "9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" "question-451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :question/id #uuid "74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81042" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" "answer-8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" "answer-d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" "answer-173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" "answer-a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" :answer/id #uuid "c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" :answer/id #uuid "8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" :answer/id #uuid "d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" :answer/id #uuid "173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer/id #uuid "a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" :question/id #uuid "d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104974" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39f466cf-09b5-5527-b854-f04096edcf50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39f466cf-09b5-5527-b854-f04096edcf50" :answer-field/id #uuid "39f466cf-09b5-5527-b854-f04096edcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" "answer-04c9d9f0-2dbe-5ac0-8036-c9df0b172d55" "answer-7cf80967-03e1-5b5a-9f33-6ed0d8781d5e" "answer-c76ffc80-3225-5c67-acc7-073ef6b43896" "answer-f191d36c-e930-5f0a-8a04-f0c1f06aec36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" :answer/id #uuid "28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04c9d9f0-2dbe-5ac0-8036-c9df0b172d55" :answer/id #uuid "04c9d9f0-2dbe-5ac0-8036-c9df0b172d55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cf80967-03e1-5b5a-9f33-6ed0d8781d5e" :answer/id #uuid "7cf80967-03e1-5b5a-9f33-6ed0d8781d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c76ffc80-3225-5c67-acc7-073ef6b43896" :answer/id #uuid "c76ffc80-3225-5c67-acc7-073ef6b43896" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f191d36c-e930-5f0a-8a04-f0c1f06aec36" :answer/id #uuid "f191d36c-e930-5f0a-8a04-f0c1f06aec36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :question/id #uuid "451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :question/math-academy-id "q-105286" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f" :answer-field/id #uuid "c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" "answer-d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" "answer-ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" "answer-c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" "answer-649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" :answer/id #uuid "f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer/id #uuid "d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" :answer/id #uuid "ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" :answer/id #uuid "c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea" :answer/id #uuid "649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-48a27db3-de33-52fc-a27c-3fb1cd88a0ab" :activity/id #uuid "48a27db3-de33-52fc-a27c-3fb1cd88a0ab" :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051048 :activity/course 17592186046668 :activity/steps ["step-1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" "step-1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" "step-5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" "step-e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" "step-6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9"] :activity/first-step "step-1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" :step/id #uuid "1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" :step/content 17592186511496 :step/next "step-1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" :step/id #uuid "1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" :step/content "question-90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" :step/next "step-5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" :step/id #uuid "5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" :step/content "question-74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :step/next "step-e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" :step/id #uuid "e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" :step/content "question-d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" :step/next "step-6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9" :step/id #uuid "6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9" :step/content "question-451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176313 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262636 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8cd55f88d614bdb49aee6eaa0ff9d1d56f6f597cd892ee199a0130b53954b5b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x+1)\\,dx+x(y+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1-(x+1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262641 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/90/902790a00a049bbfc759403125ad5f19a8c2528a46b59c721ac43fb137cd5d8e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+3}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(\\sqrt{y+3}-2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416110 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262650 :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/62/6273034b38059d3d49b2a29c91052c290151b4a29073f1c925172dd1c4c2bf72.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{2}-(-3{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}60\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =180[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =180\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =90\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-29f8ac3d-ebae-5ec0-91bf-2c2d11fca1b2" :activity/id #uuid "29f8ac3d-ebae-5ec0-91bf-2c2d11fca1b2" :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050332 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-d38c598c-e6c8-596d-b5b1-344239cf7390" "step-0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" "step-2081022a-bc13-504d-84ff-392ffcb85254" "step-af0a8f06-6ad0-508f-9f03-0538665d492d" "step-2c0549f0-8417-57bd-b711-92eea5341e9e"] :activity/first-step "step-d38c598c-e6c8-596d-b5b1-344239cf7390" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d38c598c-e6c8-596d-b5b1-344239cf7390" :step/id #uuid "d38c598c-e6c8-596d-b5b1-344239cf7390" :step/content 17592186176313 :step/next "step-0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" :step/id #uuid "0d02eb78-b31f-5714-9473-a758d6d95bda" :step/content 17592186262636 :step/next "step-2081022a-bc13-504d-84ff-392ffcb85254" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2081022a-bc13-504d-84ff-392ffcb85254" :step/id #uuid "2081022a-bc13-504d-84ff-392ffcb85254" :step/content 17592186262641 :step/next "step-af0a8f06-6ad0-508f-9f03-0538665d492d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-af0a8f06-6ad0-508f-9f03-0538665d492d" :step/id #uuid "af0a8f06-6ad0-508f-9f03-0538665d492d" :step/content 17592186416110 :step/next "step-2c0549f0-8417-57bd-b711-92eea5341e9e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c0549f0-8417-57bd-b711-92eea5341e9e" :step/id #uuid "2c0549f0-8417-57bd-b711-92eea5341e9e" :step/content 17592186262650 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115206 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115036 :question/problem "What is the domain of $f(x)=\\ln (2x+4)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y = \\ln x$ is\n\n$$\nx \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x+4$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x+4 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-4 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = f(x)$ is $x\\in\\left( -2, \\infty \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115038 :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x) = -\\ln(4x).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y = \\ln{x}$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115207 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e7e97e3-f06a-5ac7-917d-a28a5b06bd45"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115037 :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x) = \\log(2x) - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\log(2x) - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =2 \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =100 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=50.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115202 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" "question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115031 :question/problem "What is the range of $f(x) = 5\\log_6(x-1)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y = \\log_{6}(x)$ is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nFor a logarithmic function, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb" :question/id #uuid "1124f708-b647-5fc7-9069-27535f9fe6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-41247" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{7}⁡(2x-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y={log}_{7}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we replace $x$ with $2x-3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "66632494-a4ad-5cc3-8b8f-9fc6ebacf3b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5f697687-ec84-5c60-ac6b-c8ef620378f1" "answer-eafff2db-2dce-5e16-ad27-40dcf2655fe9" "answer-d4a03f4b-ffe0-5cc9-94bd-21afd40d50db" "answer-1c8bb634-5f7a-526b-b108-ac68b8ec6d79" "answer-9e80d32f-b381-5f5e-ada5-9f3b71ef09ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "an