e vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4978139-adc9-5b94-8e08-b020f6e0a655"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4978139-adc9-5b94-8e08-b020f6e0a655" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4978139-adc9-5b94-8e08-b020f6e0a655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-499e3a9f-a923-51f5-87ce-febf5570144d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-499e3a9f-a923-51f5-87ce-febf5570144d" "answer-9937c1bf-8d48-5871-8ae9-12aa552971f9" "answer-687ea3cb-6fc5-534a-986a-201d5b2e216b" "answer-5ad0934e-ac45-5e5a-966d-8d3b997ea00c" "answer-8233d628-290e-5135-b160-f3e991418b7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-499e3a9f-a923-51f5-87ce-febf5570144d" :answer/id #uuid "499e3a9f-a923-51f5-87ce-febf5570144d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9937c1bf-8d48-5871-8ae9-12aa552971f9" :answer/id #uuid "9937c1bf-8d48-5871-8ae9-12aa552971f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-687ea3cb-6fc5-534a-986a-201d5b2e216b" :answer/id #uuid "687ea3cb-6fc5-534a-986a-201d5b2e216b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ad0934e-ac45-5e5a-966d-8d3b997ea00c" :answer/id #uuid "5ad0934e-ac45-5e5a-966d-8d3b997ea00c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8233d628-290e-5135-b160-f3e991418b7d" :answer/id #uuid "8233d628-290e-5135-b160-f3e991418b7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e7e97e3-f06a-5ac7-917d-a28a5b06bd45" :question/id #uuid "1e7e97e3-f06a-5ac7-917d-a28a5b06bd45" :question/math-academy-id "q-81997" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7)=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+7)} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 2x+7 & =1 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6088b2f7-2581-5b62-9b22-3ffa46f44d91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6088b2f7-2581-5b62-9b22-3ffa46f44d91" :answer-field/id #uuid "6088b2f7-2581-5b62-9b22-3ffa46f44d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f937e83-c00e-5e24-b941-669dcd4af536" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54dbec5e-bb89-52c0-ab0f-690a484002ff" "answer-2f937e83-c00e-5e24-b941-669dcd4af536" "answer-f027a5aa-0171-5629-ac7d-daef459b821c" "answer-ef8be49d-6b79-527f-8dc8-5e1926470d59" "answer-4a2d223a-b4c3-59b7-8f03-696f806b5729"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54dbec5e-bb89-52c0-ab0f-690a484002ff" :answer/id #uuid "54dbec5e-bb89-52c0-ab0f-690a484002ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f937e83-c00e-5e24-b941-669dcd4af536" :answer/id #uuid "2f937e83-c00e-5e24-b941-669dcd4af536" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f027a5aa-0171-5629-ac7d-daef459b821c" :answer/id #uuid "f027a5aa-0171-5629-ac7d-daef459b821c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef8be49d-6b79-527f-8dc8-5e1926470d59" :answer/id #uuid "ef8be49d-6b79-527f-8dc8-5e1926470d59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2d223a-b4c3-59b7-8f03-696f806b5729" :answer/id #uuid "4a2d223a-b4c3-59b7-8f03-696f806b5729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234440 :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-6 & =0 \\\\ 2{log}_{2}⁡(x) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x) & =3 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x)} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8.$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "9768eaaa-8761-51c7-87c0-8475f53214f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" "answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" "answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" "answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" "answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/id #uuid "f0704e6b-a2ce-5b70-bceb-9f22d2c7b3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/id #uuid "ed15a969-b552-5a08-90d0-2f86afcf0b42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/id #uuid "d054c24f-b046-5e4b-9c2e-39da68f8ec3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/id #uuid "ab806a45-cc37-5501-bd80-db533a934d1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/id #uuid "e97016e4-6a8f-5328-b33e-52efd1dc2a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/id #uuid "800d4066-ddae-513f-a0b6-998ce2ab2521" :question/math-academy-id "q-81862" :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{2}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/id #uuid "607549e3-c7c2-556c-bfd6-1c5bdeb01a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" "answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" "answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" "answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" "answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" :answer/id #uuid "b848b9ad-d1ad-5380-b3cf-6833aab59f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" :answer/id #uuid "965f1838-da64-5428-9b89-29a1f523deb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" :answer/id #uuid "1e0ed534-4003-583b-b0cb-07a91f0d3599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer/id #uuid "20d66e1a-2b6a-5c47-a4b1-f400a0483cb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce" :answer/id #uuid "2c73133c-2ea2-5288-afe7-f8e114b0bfce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/id #uuid "cf10c890-1bed-5075-9d7f-bb216357cf94" :question/math-academy-id "q-166551" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/id #uuid "55b3c41d-9ab9-5b46-9a5f-0309beda7ca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" "answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" "answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" "answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" "answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" :answer/id #uuid "91ac72ca-94cc-556f-9e0b-0e02e4fa7c7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" :answer/id #uuid "294c8be5-9207-557e-bc83-1962a7384a81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" :answer/id #uuid "cf8874f7-b734-5002-8cd2-d18ad56523c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" :answer/id #uuid "6667b192-562c-5e48-8727-d1811a01ade7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer/id #uuid "6138d91b-8219-58ef-a46d-5a53d78fb785" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116480 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = \\log_2(x-1).$ What is the domain of $f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f1/f135e52b40df0923dea0bfd58a3255a096cab4ab23faa7c761ac5998fdfdd782.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the domain of $y=\\log_a (x)$ is\n\n$$\nx > 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x),$ we take the above inequality and replace $x$ with the argument (or input) of the logarithm function contained in $f(x).$ In this case, the argument is $x-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in(1, \\infty).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116527 :tutorial/content "When we transform a logarithmic function $y = \\log_a (x),$ its range is not affected.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed logarithmic function $f(x) = 3\\log_2(x-1)+1.$ \n\n![](images/4a/4a5bad1159efe184b0afc2731ebd6b5dd6149a0a1ba2d530cfdc769ab2acf250.png)\n\nThe range of $\\log_2 x$ is\n\n$$\n\\log_2 x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBecause the range of the logarithm function is the interval containing all real numbers, horizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect its range. Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115436 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186118059 :answer-field/choices 17592186118064] [:db/retract 17592186118059 :answer-field/choices 17592186118060] [:db/retract 17592186118059 :answer-field/choices 17592186118061] [:db/retract 17592186118059 :answer-field/choices 17592186118062] [:db/retract 17592186118059 :answer-field/choices 17592186118063] {:db/id 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-de9e1bb6-8c29-536b-b329-05255b6d1f04" "question-34925275-ae4c-5d65-95f3-737bbc060369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-de9e1bb6-8c29-536b-b329-05255b6d1f04" :question/id #uuid "de9e1bb6-8c29-536b-b329-05255b6d1f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-75636" :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd435012-1099-531b-992e-08104da3eb22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd435012-1099-531b-992e-08104da3eb22" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd435012-1099-531b-992e-08104da3eb22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68f85d0f-2150-5cfc-8c7b-b48fb1678f4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8df14e3b-06f0-5de6-a3f3-c95b09e3bf3f" "answer-0a561db8-da05-5488-90b2-b1499bdfd024" "answer-68f85d0f-2150-5cfc-8c7b-b48fb1678f4d" "answer-14137556-54c3-5d40-a07b-5a7c1bb6e3a5" "answer-62097d8f-b2fe-5ff4-a7b0-e3bbc5636bd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8df14e3b-06f0-5de6-a3f3-c95b09e3bf3f" :answer/id #uuid "8df14e3b-06f0-5de6-a3f3-c95b09e3bf3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a561db8-da05-5488-90b2-b1499bdfd024" :answer/id #uuid "0a561db8-da05-5488-90b2-b1499bdfd024" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68f85d0f-2150-5cfc-8c7b-b48fb1678f4d" :answer/id #uuid "68f85d0f-2150-5cfc-8c7b-b48fb1678f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14137556-54c3-5d40-a07b-5a7c1bb6e3a5" :answer/id #uuid "14137556-54c3-5d40-a07b-5a7c1bb6e3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62097d8f-b2fe-5ff4-a7b0-e3bbc5636bd0" :answer/id #uuid "62097d8f-b2fe-5ff4-a7b0-e3bbc5636bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120320 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=-4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=-4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118059 :answer-field/correct "answer-d8744d9d-da34-506f-bcf4-460c113a16e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e97f2413-80bb-5bcf-bb04-ab19ba48bdaa" "answer-d8744d9d-da34-506f-bcf4-460c113a16e2" "answer-a4632e22-167c-5056-9f27-8ad30a2dcc0f" "answer-02117761-522d-518e-9380-cd3df0b411cd" "answer-8680a3ba-b8c5-5a4d-bba9-8c97bc9e294a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e97f2413-80bb-5bcf-bb04-ab19ba48bdaa" :answer/id #uuid "e97f2413-80bb-5bcf-bb04-ab19ba48bdaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8744d9d-da34-506f-bcf4-460c113a16e2" :answer/id #uuid "d8744d9d-da34-506f-bcf4-460c113a16e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4632e22-167c-5056-9f27-8ad30a2dcc0f" :answer/id #uuid "a4632e22-167c-5056-9f27-8ad30a2dcc0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02117761-522d-518e-9380-cd3df0b411cd" :answer/id #uuid "02117761-522d-518e-9380-cd3df0b411cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8680a3ba-b8c5-5a4d-bba9-8c97bc9e294a" :answer/id #uuid "8680a3ba-b8c5-5a4d-bba9-8c97bc9e294a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34925275-ae4c-5d65-95f3-737bbc060369" :question/id #uuid "34925275-ae4c-5d65-95f3-737bbc060369" :question/math-academy-id "q-75642" :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d31dd379-95e8-5e67-883a-a09641f1eecc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d31dd379-95e8-5e67-883a-a09641f1eecc" :answer-field/id #uuid "d31dd379-95e8-5e67-883a-a09641f1eecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04f0f6d1-8402-5397-ac16-a4b89f34b7f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35f96482-abb5-57cb-8ffa-3db8da48fb19" "answer-04f0f6d1-8402-5397-ac16-a4b89f34b7f9" "answer-2cc05134-02b1-5727-8502-07a1f3d7d9e6" "answer-19a0357a-ce57-58ed-bfb1-8181d4484baa" "answer-de9cfaf8-1939-5e03-9914-e2205bc5fb25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35f96482-abb5-57cb-8ffa-3db8da48fb19" :answer/id #uuid "35f96482-abb5-57cb-8ffa-3db8da48fb19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04f0f6d1-8402-5397-ac16-a4b89f34b7f9" :answer/id #uuid "04f0f6d1-8402-5397-ac16-a4b89f34b7f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cc05134-02b1-5727-8502-07a1f3d7d9e6" :answer/id #uuid "2cc05134-02b1-5727-8502-07a1f3d7d9e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19a0357a-ce57-58ed-bfb1-8181d4484baa" :answer/id #uuid "19a0357a-ce57-58ed-bfb1-8181d4484baa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de9cfaf8-1939-5e03-9914-e2205bc5fb25" :answer/id #uuid "de9cfaf8-1939-5e03-9914-e2205bc5fb25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881" :question/id #uuid "848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881" :question/math-academy-id "q-157254" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e84dcffa-6797-50d5-aaea-ce1046d2837d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e84dcffa-6797-50d5-aaea-ce1046d2837d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e84dcffa-6797-50d5-aaea-ce1046d2837d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d59668a-5b18-5b17-be7f-d90767135530" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0248fac4-7021-5d3c-8fe6-630d74a3d1b5" "answer-de17d5bf-c3c6-5996-a382-6447bcc6e98a" "answer-e5988f07-d1dc-55e6-8850-b106bbc457fe" "answer-a19b5317-af1f-54a5-aa91-38cce6394e14" "answer-2d59668a-5b18-5b17-be7f-d90767135530"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0248fac4-7021-5d3c-8fe6-630d74a3d1b5" :answer/id #uuid "0248fac4-7021-5d3c-8fe6-630d74a3d1b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de17d5bf-c3c6-5996-a382-6447bcc6e98a" :answer/id #uuid "de17d5bf-c3c6-5996-a382-6447bcc6e98a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5988f07-d1dc-55e6-8850-b106bbc457fe" :answer/id #uuid "e5988f07-d1dc-55e6-8850-b106bbc457fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a19b5317-af1f-54a5-aa91-38cce6394e14" :answer/id #uuid "a19b5317-af1f-54a5-aa91-38cce6394e14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d59668a-5b18-5b17-be7f-d90767135530" :answer/id #uuid "2d59668a-5b18-5b17-be7f-d90767135530" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997" :question/id #uuid "5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997" :question/math-academy-id "q-105317" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11e59e1d-92dd-58fa-bf22-f25988693e4e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11e59e1d-92dd-58fa-bf22-f25988693e4e" :answer-field/id #uuid "11e59e1d-92dd-58fa-bf22-f25988693e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09b93e02-911f-5448-ab37-cc2c74c1efa2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e608ec51-2291-5759-a881-171cd19404a6" "answer-2aa4ebb0-8d31-5131-bf0e-7bb725b939ee" "answer-09b93e02-911f-5448-ab37-cc2c74c1efa2" "answer-3fe615b5-1980-5ec3-8f2a-a28dfe3cf0c6" "answer-466b980c-756d-5db4-8e7c-797a12ae9d1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e608ec51-2291-5759-a881-171cd19404a6" :answer/id #uuid "e608ec51-2291-5759-a881-171cd19404a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa4ebb0-8d31-5131-bf0e-7bb725b939ee" :answer/id #uuid "2aa4ebb0-8d31-5131-bf0e-7bb725b939ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09b93e02-911f-5448-ab37-cc2c74c1efa2" :answer/id #uuid "09b93e02-911f-5448-ab37-cc2c74c1efa2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fe615b5-1980-5ec3-8f2a-a28dfe3cf0c6" :answer/id #uuid "3fe615b5-1980-5ec3-8f2a-a28dfe3cf0c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-466b980c-756d-5db4-8e7c-797a12ae9d1a" :answer/id #uuid "466b980c-756d-5db4-8e7c-797a12ae9d1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115410 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97cef597-7bfc-539c-89dd-d586cabec0a9" "question-a800f321-defb-572d-a55b-71be8add2663"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-97cef597-7bfc-539c-89dd-d586cabec0a9" :question/id #uuid "97cef597-7bfc-539c-89dd-d586cabec0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118706" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b3636b4a0534a8794c7b7955aa16d8bb90d98dcdaebbc40311c2e13e971e7af.png)\n\nGiven $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}?$" :question/worked-solution "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/94/94e65bdbb5e82c72b97f69ab2da1b16e2efe8399a83e78a2721d1e74eaa9b8b4.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $Q$ to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa3254d24cd0d21a8a78009c8c07591e34ab46fa0449fc27e179d5a32ac70a17.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb3a80b-5e49-595e-8543-da11372ae2a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb3a80b-5e49-595e-8543-da11372ae2a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb3a80b-5e49-595e-8543-da11372ae2a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09d7bbb4-3bc2-51db-bc57-a7edc95b5b12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e22f0b89-ef15-50d8-9928-9838ddb470b7" "answer-c4ee513f-9682-5bc9-85b8-9ce4afe58e11" "answer-749fefbd-6ad9-5a5f-8a13-394b61b686ee" "answer-09d7bbb4-3bc2-51db-bc57-a7edc95b5b12" "answer-bf16add8-dafb-5323-b37a-182bc79b